1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,006
On the morning of June tenth, nineteen twelve,

2
00:00:03,006 --> 00:00:05,063
a mother in southwest Iowa

3
00:00:05,063 --> 00:00:06,803
telephoned a neighbor's house.

4
00:00:06,803 --> 00:00:09,493
Her husband told the coroner about it.

5
00:00:09,493 --> 00:00:11,234
My wife did, yes.

6
00:00:11,234 --> 00:00:13,607
He could not say what time.

7
00:00:13,607 --> 00:00:15,347
She tried, he said,

8
00:00:15,347 --> 00:00:17,088
about three different times.

9
00:00:17,088 --> 00:00:20,094
Her two daughters had stayed the night there.

10
00:00:20,094 --> 00:00:21,835
She expected them back.

11
00:00:22,942 --> 00:00:24,683
That house was locked.

12
00:00:24,683 --> 00:00:26,423
The blinds were down.

13
00:00:26,423 --> 00:00:28,480
Everyone who tells this story

14
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:31,487
will tell you why it was never solved.

15
00:00:31,487 --> 00:00:33,860
A crowd walked through the rooms.

16
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:35,600
The evidence was lost.

17
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:37,657
And that is partly true.

18
00:00:37,657 --> 00:00:40,347
But here is what the paperwork shows.

19
00:00:40,347 --> 00:00:43,354
This case did not run out of evidence.

20
00:00:43,354 --> 00:00:44,778
For nineteen years

21
00:00:44,778 --> 00:00:46,518
it kept getting more.

22
00:00:46,518 --> 00:00:47,626
A closet.

23
00:00:47,626 --> 00:00:51,265
A photograph, said to come from inside a child's eye.

24
00:00:51,265 --> 00:00:53,955
A sound that turned into a man.

25
00:00:53,955 --> 00:00:55,379
A signed confession.

26
00:00:55,379 --> 00:00:56,486
A fingerprint.

27
00:00:56,486 --> 00:01:00,125
I can't tell you who killed eight people in Villisca.

28
00:01:00,125 --> 00:01:01,550
No one can.

29
00:01:01,550 --> 00:01:04,556
What I can give you is an inventory.

30
00:01:04,556 --> 00:01:06,613
What was in that house.

31
00:01:06,613 --> 00:01:08,986
And what was carried in afterward.

32
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,750
The first entry is the people.

33
00:01:11,750 --> 00:01:13,767
Joe Moore was forty-three.

34
00:01:13,767 --> 00:01:15,784
He sold farm implements.

35
00:01:15,784 --> 00:01:17,801
Sarah Moore was thirty-nine,

36
00:01:17,801 --> 00:01:21,285
and she helped run the Children's Day program

37
00:01:21,285 --> 00:01:23,302
at the Presbyterian church.

38
00:01:23,302 --> 00:01:24,952
Herman was eleven.

39
00:01:24,952 --> 00:01:26,603
That Sunday night,

40
00:01:26,603 --> 00:01:28,620
he spoke a piece

41
00:01:28,620 --> 00:01:31,370
about God watching over the sparrows.

42
00:01:31,370 --> 00:01:33,021
Katherine was ten.

43
00:01:33,021 --> 00:01:34,671
Boyd was seven.

44
00:01:34,671 --> 00:01:36,321
Paul was five.

45
00:01:36,321 --> 00:01:39,805
Lena Stillinger took part in the program too.

46
00:01:39,805 --> 00:01:42,556
Her sister Ina was with her.

47
00:01:42,556 --> 00:01:45,306
The records disagree about their ages.

48
00:01:45,306 --> 00:01:47,690
Lena was eleven or twelve.

49
00:01:47,690 --> 00:01:50,074
Ina was eight or nine,

50
00:01:50,074 --> 00:01:52,824
and one trial report says six.

51
00:01:52,824 --> 00:01:55,208
At six o'clock that evening,

52
00:01:55,208 --> 00:01:57,959
according to their older sister Blanche,

53
00:01:57,959 --> 00:02:00,709
Joe Moore telephoned the Stillinger home.

54
00:02:00,709 --> 00:02:05,293
He did not want the girls walking back in the dark.

55
00:02:05,293 --> 00:02:08,044
Could they stay with his family?

56
00:02:08,044 --> 00:02:11,895
Blanche said she thought that would be all right.

57
00:02:11,895 --> 00:02:15,745
The pastor testified the program ended about nine fifteen.

58
00:02:15,745 --> 00:02:18,496
Nobody who saw what came next

59
00:02:18,496 --> 00:02:21,246
ever gave an account that held.

60
00:02:21,246 --> 00:02:23,997
So I won't give you one.

61
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:25,044
Monday morning.

62
00:02:25,044 --> 00:02:26,687
A neighbor, Mary Peckham,

63
00:02:26,687 --> 00:02:28,926
noticed how still the house was.

64
00:02:28,926 --> 00:02:30,568
She tried the doors.

65
00:02:30,568 --> 00:02:33,106
Joe's brother Ross came with a key.

66
00:02:33,106 --> 00:02:36,539
He opened the door to the room off the parlor.

67
00:02:36,539 --> 00:02:38,182
He told the coroner

68
00:02:38,182 --> 00:02:41,615
he did not wait long enough to see anything else.

69
00:02:41,615 --> 00:02:44,750
The town marshal, Hank Horton, went through every room.

70
00:02:44,750 --> 00:02:46,691
And then he did something

71
00:02:46,691 --> 00:02:48,034
almost nobody retells.

72
00:02:48,034 --> 00:02:50,274
According to the Moore store clerk,

73
00:02:50,274 --> 00:02:51,916
he locked the house

74
00:02:51,916 --> 00:02:54,454
and left the keys with the neighbor.

75
00:02:54,454 --> 00:02:55,499
Remember that.

76
00:02:55,499 --> 00:02:56,842
A locked house.

77
00:02:56,842 --> 00:02:59,380
A woman next door, holding the key.

78
00:02:59,380 --> 00:03:02,216
It is the last moment anyone could say

79
00:03:02,216 --> 00:03:03,858
who had been inside.

80
00:03:03,858 --> 00:03:05,799
When the doctors went in,

81
00:03:05,799 --> 00:03:07,441
every face was covered.

82
00:03:07,441 --> 00:03:09,382
Dr. Williams climbed the stairs

83
00:03:09,382 --> 00:03:11,920
to the room on the south side.

84
00:03:11,920 --> 00:03:14,159
A small boy, on his stomach.

85
00:03:14,159 --> 00:03:15,503
A gauze undershirt

86
00:03:15,503 --> 00:03:18,040
laid over the top of his head.

87
00:03:18,040 --> 00:03:21,175
Dr. Williams told the inquest what he did next.

88
00:03:22,220 --> 00:03:23,863
I lifted that off

89
00:03:23,863 --> 00:03:25,206
lifted it off

90
00:03:25,206 --> 00:03:27,445
to see which one it was.

91
00:03:32,458 --> 00:03:34,761
So here is the first inventory.

92
00:03:34,761 --> 00:03:38,599
An ax, near the bed where Lena and Ina were found.

93
00:03:38,599 --> 00:03:41,823
A piece of bacon, wrapped in a flour sack,

94
00:03:41,823 --> 00:03:44,126
at the head of that bed.

95
00:03:44,126 --> 00:03:48,272
A lamp on the floor, at the foot of the parents' bed.

96
00:03:48,272 --> 00:03:51,189
The marshal moved it out of the way.

97
00:03:51,189 --> 00:03:53,799
Skirts and aprons hung over the windows.

98
00:03:53,799 --> 00:03:55,488
Marks in the ceiling.

99
00:03:55,488 --> 00:03:57,484
Dr. Williams found no footprints.

100
00:03:57,484 --> 00:03:58,866
Five years later,

101
00:03:58,866 --> 00:04:00,247
the coroner testified

102
00:04:00,247 --> 00:04:03,472
there were no finger marks on the ax handle.

103
00:04:03,472 --> 00:04:05,775
Then the house was opened again.

104
00:04:05,775 --> 00:04:08,692
The National Guard came to hold the line.

105
00:04:08,692 --> 00:04:10,688
One account says ten thirty.

106
00:04:10,688 --> 00:04:12,069
Another says noon.

107
00:04:12,069 --> 00:04:15,908
How many people went in before that, I can't tell you.

108
00:04:15,908 --> 00:04:17,904
Later accounts say one hundred.

109
00:04:17,904 --> 00:04:19,286
Others say hundreds.

110
00:04:19,286 --> 00:04:22,510
I have not found a count from that day.

111
00:04:22,510 --> 00:04:24,506
What the record does show

112
00:04:24,506 --> 00:04:27,116
is a house entered again and again.

113
00:04:27,116 --> 00:04:30,033
Jessie Moore testified she went in that day,

114
00:04:30,033 --> 00:04:32,643
to get family pictures for the press.

115
00:04:32,643 --> 00:04:34,025
Bedding was burned.

116
00:04:34,025 --> 00:04:36,328
The house was cleaned, then repapered.

117
00:04:36,328 --> 00:04:37,402
So yes.

118
00:04:37,402 --> 00:04:38,784
Things were lost.

119
00:04:38,784 --> 00:04:41,087
But loss only explains what disappeared.

120
00:04:41,087 --> 00:04:43,390
It cannot explain what showed up.

121
00:04:43,375 --> 00:04:46,850
Item one arrives five days after the murders.

122
00:04:46,850 --> 00:04:49,229
A newspaper report, datelined Omaha.

123
00:04:49,229 --> 00:04:51,607
A woman named Retta Johnson

124
00:04:51,607 --> 00:04:53,985
has come back from Villisca.

125
00:04:53,985 --> 00:04:56,730
There is a closet, she says,

126
00:04:56,730 --> 00:05:00,205
off the room where the Stillinger girls slept.

127
00:05:00,205 --> 00:05:02,584
Bags of cotton batting inside.

128
00:05:02,584 --> 00:05:05,694
On them, the marks of a man

129
00:05:05,694 --> 00:05:08,438
who had sat and stood there.

130
00:05:08,438 --> 00:05:10,816
And in the girls' bed,

131
00:05:10,816 --> 00:05:13,195
a piece of watch chain.

132
00:05:13,195 --> 00:05:15,207
Now follow the closet.

133
00:05:15,207 --> 00:05:17,951
By the trial, five years on,

134
00:05:17,951 --> 00:05:21,427
the list of exhibits names a closet upstairs.

135
00:05:21,427 --> 00:05:25,269
A public comment posted under historian Edgar Epperly's name

136
00:05:25,269 --> 00:05:29,843
traces a version of the story to a Kansas City reporter,

137
00:05:29,843 --> 00:05:33,685
with cigarettes, and a heel print in the dust.

138
00:05:33,685 --> 00:05:36,063
It says an undertaker checked,

139
00:05:36,063 --> 00:05:40,271
and the box of cotton sat on two suit boxes

140
00:05:40,271 --> 00:05:42,649
that had not been crushed.

141
00:05:42,649 --> 00:05:46,125
Today, you will hear it was the attic.

142
00:05:46,125 --> 00:05:48,137
Now follow the chain.

143
00:05:48,137 --> 00:05:51,613
A watch chain, in a bed, in June.

144
00:05:51,613 --> 00:05:53,260
By the trial,

145
00:05:53,260 --> 00:05:55,638
a piece of key chain,

146
00:05:55,638 --> 00:05:58,748
and Joe Moore's watch, on the floor.

147
00:05:58,748 --> 00:06:01,126
The containers stayed the same.

148
00:06:01,126 --> 00:06:03,871
What was inside them kept changing.

149
00:06:03,874 --> 00:06:05,514
August, nineteen twelve.

150
00:06:05,514 --> 00:06:09,340
A man from Villisca tells people in Council Bluffs

151
00:06:09,340 --> 00:06:12,802
that detectives have a photograph of the killer.

152
00:06:12,802 --> 00:06:15,170
They got it, he says,

153
00:06:15,170 --> 00:06:18,996
from the retina of one of the Stillinger sisters.

154
00:06:18,996 --> 00:06:22,822
I have found no such photograph in any record.

155
00:06:22,822 --> 00:06:25,919
Her parents were alive to read that.

156
00:06:25,919 --> 00:06:28,652
By the end of that year,

157
00:06:28,652 --> 00:06:33,206
newspapers had reported at least five different men held or arrested.

158
00:06:33,206 --> 00:06:34,482
A brother-in-law.

159
00:06:34,482 --> 00:06:36,850
A man with stained shoes.

160
00:06:36,850 --> 00:06:39,947
A farmhand who wrote about a vision.

161
00:06:39,947 --> 00:06:41,951
A Sioux City porter,

162
00:06:41,951 --> 00:06:45,049
whom the headline identified by his race.

163
00:06:45,049 --> 00:06:46,324
A farmer.

164
00:06:46,324 --> 00:06:50,150
None of them was convicted of anything at Villisca.

165
00:06:50,150 --> 00:06:54,340
The question had stopped being what happened in the house.

166
00:06:54,340 --> 00:06:55,980
It had become

167
00:06:55,980 --> 00:06:59,077
who was going to pay for it.

168
00:06:59,083 --> 00:07:00,618
In nineteen sixteen,

169
00:07:00,618 --> 00:07:03,177
a private detective named James Wilkerson

170
00:07:03,177 --> 00:07:05,395
brought the case a theory.

171
00:07:05,395 --> 00:07:07,271
State senator Frank Jones,

172
00:07:07,271 --> 00:07:10,171
Joe Moore's old employer and business rival,

173
00:07:10,171 --> 00:07:12,047
had hired a killer.

174
00:07:12,047 --> 00:07:14,265
Wilkerson's man was William Mansfield.

175
00:07:14,265 --> 00:07:17,165
A grand jury refused to indict him.

176
00:07:17,165 --> 00:07:20,065
Frank Jones was never charged with anything.

177
00:07:20,065 --> 00:07:22,283
He sued Wilkerson for slander,

178
00:07:22,283 --> 00:07:24,500
asked for sixty thousand dollars,

179
00:07:24,500 --> 00:07:25,694
and lost.

180
00:07:25,694 --> 00:07:27,571
And in that lawsuit,

181
00:07:27,571 --> 00:07:30,812
a witness named Ed Landers took the oath.

182
00:07:30,812 --> 00:07:33,030
You have met Landers already,

183
00:07:33,030 --> 00:07:34,565
in a way.

184
00:07:34,565 --> 00:07:38,147
He was staying a few houses from the Moores.

185
00:07:38,147 --> 00:07:40,706
At the inquest in nineteen twelve,

186
00:07:40,706 --> 00:07:43,947
he described something he heard that Sunday night.

187
00:07:43,947 --> 00:07:45,141
A sound.

188
00:07:45,141 --> 00:07:47,700
Like one boy hooting for another.

189
00:07:47,700 --> 00:07:51,282
The next day, he said, it occurred to him

190
00:07:51,282 --> 00:07:54,182
it might have been a woman moaning.

191
00:07:54,182 --> 00:07:57,082
In the published summary of that testimony,

192
00:07:57,082 --> 00:07:59,300
that is all there is.

193
00:07:59,300 --> 00:08:00,835
Four years later,

194
00:08:00,835 --> 00:08:02,029
under oath,

195
00:08:02,029 --> 00:08:04,929
he said that on that same night

196
00:08:04,929 --> 00:08:07,488
he saw Frank Jones's son, Albert,

197
00:08:07,488 --> 00:08:09,706
go into the Moore home.

198
00:08:10,900 --> 00:08:13,459
Albert Jones was never charged either.

199
00:08:13,459 --> 00:08:16,359
Both men denied any part in it.

200
00:08:16,359 --> 00:08:19,600
The prosecution attacked Landers's account in open court.

201
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:20,794
A sound

202
00:08:20,794 --> 00:08:22,329
became a man.

203
00:08:27,333 --> 00:08:30,881
In nineteen seventeen, the state found its own document.

204
00:08:30,881 --> 00:08:33,078
The Reverend Lyn George Kelly

205
00:08:33,078 --> 00:08:34,937
was a traveling preacher,

206
00:08:34,937 --> 00:08:37,133
in town for one Sunday.

207
00:08:37,133 --> 00:08:40,682
He was charged with the murder of Lena Stillinger.

208
00:08:40,682 --> 00:08:43,892
He had been charged with mailing obscene letters,

209
00:08:43,892 --> 00:08:47,779
and he had been held in a federal mental hospital.

210
00:08:47,779 --> 00:08:50,989
He caught the five nineteen train that morning.

211
00:08:50,989 --> 00:08:54,538
The train left hours before anyone opened the door.

212
00:08:54,538 --> 00:08:57,410
Witnesses swore he spoke of the murders

213
00:08:57,410 --> 00:08:59,607
before the bodies were found.

214
00:08:59,607 --> 00:09:02,141
They swore it five years later.

215
00:09:02,141 --> 00:09:06,366
A station ticket record backed up the date of one encounter.

216
00:09:06,366 --> 00:09:09,238
It could not record what was said.

217
00:09:09,238 --> 00:09:10,759
Then, Logan jail.

218
00:09:10,759 --> 00:09:11,942
August thirtieth.

219
00:09:11,942 --> 00:09:13,801
A newspaper editor testified

220
00:09:13,801 --> 00:09:17,349
that he was handcuffed and placed in Kelly's cell,

221
00:09:17,349 --> 00:09:19,208
playing an automobile thief.

222
00:09:19,208 --> 00:09:21,404
A sheriff read out accusations.

223
00:09:21,404 --> 00:09:23,601
The editor recalled eighty-seven paragraphs.

224
00:09:23,601 --> 00:09:24,784
Kelly said,

225
00:09:24,784 --> 00:09:26,305
that's a lie.

226
00:09:26,305 --> 00:09:30,529
Early the next morning, a county attorney took down a statement.

227
00:09:30,529 --> 00:09:33,402
Its first lines say it was made

228
00:09:33,402 --> 00:09:35,598
without any promise or threats.

229
00:09:35,598 --> 00:09:39,147
In it, a voice tells Kelly to slay utterly.

230
00:09:39,147 --> 00:09:42,695
He climbs the stairs, thinking they are Jacob's ladder.

231
00:09:42,695 --> 00:09:45,568
He kills the children first, it says.

232
00:09:45,568 --> 00:09:47,088
Then the parents.

233
00:09:47,088 --> 00:09:50,299
No record I have read can tell you

234
00:09:50,299 --> 00:09:53,171
whether that is the order it happened.

235
00:09:53,171 --> 00:09:55,706
The attorney general told the jury

236
00:09:55,706 --> 00:09:58,241
Kelly had been asked fifteen times

237
00:09:58,241 --> 00:10:00,775
whether he killed the parents first.

238
00:10:00,775 --> 00:10:03,310
And fifteen times, he said no.

239
00:10:03,310 --> 00:10:07,196
Twenty-five times, he was told no promise could be made.

240
00:10:07,196 --> 00:10:10,745
Fifty-one times, he was told to tell the truth.

241
00:10:10,745 --> 00:10:12,603
The state counted those.

242
00:10:12,603 --> 00:10:16,152
It offered them as proof that he was free.

243
00:10:16,152 --> 00:10:18,011
At trial, Kelly said

244
00:10:18,011 --> 00:10:21,221
I have no recollection of signing that paper.

245
00:10:21,221 --> 00:10:23,418
The record shows a signature.

246
00:10:23,418 --> 00:10:26,966
It cannot show you what the signature is worth.

247
00:10:26,958 --> 00:10:30,523
There is one more name in the Omaha paper.

248
00:10:30,523 --> 00:10:33,749
A committee was raising money for Kelly's defense.

249
00:10:33,749 --> 00:10:35,278
It held meetings.

250
00:10:35,278 --> 00:10:37,145
It passed the hat.

251
00:10:37,145 --> 00:10:39,013
Its officers are listed.

252
00:10:39,013 --> 00:10:41,899
Remember the man who told the coroner

253
00:10:41,899 --> 00:10:43,767
my wife did, yes.

254
00:10:43,767 --> 00:10:46,314
She phoned about three different times.

255
00:10:46,314 --> 00:10:49,201
The state of Iowa had charged Kelly

256
00:10:49,201 --> 00:10:51,068
with killing his daughter.

257
00:10:52,257 --> 00:10:53,785
And Joseph Stillinger

258
00:10:53,785 --> 00:10:56,332
was the president of the committee

259
00:10:56,332 --> 00:10:58,539
trying to set him free.

260
00:11:03,541 --> 00:11:06,242
The paper does not say why.

261
00:11:06,242 --> 00:11:10,742
The committee said it wanted the guilty man brought to justice.

262
00:11:10,742 --> 00:11:13,802
It did not believe it had him.

263
00:11:13,802 --> 00:11:16,503
The first jury took twenty ballots.

264
00:11:16,503 --> 00:11:18,843
Eleven for acquittal, every time.

265
00:11:18,843 --> 00:11:24,064
One man, W. C. Brown, held for not guilty by reason of insanity.

266
00:11:24,064 --> 00:11:27,124
In November, a second jury acquitted Kelly.

267
00:11:27,124 --> 00:11:29,464
He was found not guilty.

268
00:11:29,464 --> 00:11:31,445
That is the verdict.

269
00:11:31,445 --> 00:11:33,785
There is one last item.

270
00:11:33,785 --> 00:11:35,405
March, nineteen thirty-one.

271
00:11:35,405 --> 00:11:38,106
A man in a Detroit jail,

272
00:11:38,106 --> 00:11:40,446
after five hours with detectives,

273
00:11:40,446 --> 00:11:42,786
confesses to the Villisca murders.

274
00:11:42,786 --> 00:11:44,406
The papers report

275
00:11:44,406 --> 00:11:48,187
that his fingerprints matched prints found at the scene.

276
00:11:48,187 --> 00:11:49,807
Remember the coroner.

277
00:11:49,807 --> 00:11:52,507
No finger marks on the handle.

278
00:11:52,507 --> 00:11:55,568
And this man's confession has a problem.

279
00:11:55,568 --> 00:11:58,268
He says he killed a man,

280
00:11:58,268 --> 00:11:59,528
his wife,

281
00:11:59,528 --> 00:12:01,508
and their four children.

282
00:12:01,508 --> 00:12:02,769
Six people.

283
00:12:02,769 --> 00:12:05,469
Officers said it did not tally.

284
00:12:05,469 --> 00:12:08,169
The last confession in the file

285
00:12:08,169 --> 00:12:10,150
forgot Lena and Ina.

286
00:12:15,166 --> 00:12:17,731
So here is the inventory now.

287
00:12:17,731 --> 00:12:18,928
A house.

288
00:12:18,928 --> 00:12:20,125
An ax.

289
00:12:20,125 --> 00:12:21,665
A few photographs.

290
00:12:21,665 --> 00:12:23,888
Everything else was carried in.

291
00:12:23,888 --> 00:12:27,479
Some of it, by people with something to gain.

292
00:12:27,479 --> 00:12:29,702
Much of it, I think,

293
00:12:29,702 --> 00:12:34,319
by people who could not stand an empty line on the form.

294
00:12:34,319 --> 00:12:37,568
That is what this kind of crime does.

295
00:12:37,568 --> 00:12:41,502
It leaves a space the exact size of a name.

296
00:12:41,502 --> 00:12:44,751
And a town will put anything in it.

297
00:12:44,751 --> 00:12:45,948
A stranger.

298
00:12:45,948 --> 00:12:47,145
A senator.

299
00:12:47,145 --> 00:12:49,368
A preacher who heard voices.

300
00:12:49,368 --> 00:12:52,959
No one has ever been convicted of these murders.

301
00:12:52,959 --> 00:12:54,840
Reverend Kelly was acquitted.

302
00:12:54,840 --> 00:12:57,748
Frank and Albert Jones were never charged.

303
00:12:57,748 --> 00:13:00,313
The one thing in this file

304
00:13:00,313 --> 00:13:02,536
that nobody needed to invent

305
00:13:02,536 --> 00:13:04,075
is a mother,

306
00:13:04,075 --> 00:13:05,956
on a Monday morning,

307
00:13:05,956 --> 00:13:07,495
calling a house.

308
00:13:07,495 --> 00:13:09,376
About three different times.

309
00:13:10,573 --> 00:13:15,191
This film is drawn from court testimony and newspapers of the time.

310
00:13:15,191 --> 00:13:17,414
A report is not proof.

311
00:13:17,414 --> 00:13:21,005
Everyone named here is presumed innocent of these murders.
